I read this group with a full expectation of being spoiled.  The only
question is to what extent I choose to be spoiled, because although I
am looking for hints and help I am not looking for my game to play
itself ... I still like to make my own way around.  Of course, after
five thousand games, one is likely to have learned a lot anyway.

Consider a fishing club, made up of sporting fishermen of various
backgrounds, experience and dedication to the pursuit.  Each one has
his favourite fishing hole or stretch or river, and each through his
experience (and perhaps a little scientific research) has a storehouse
of tricks and tips.  BUT, this fishing club forbids the sharing of
tricks and tips at its meetings.  What are the members going to
discuss? - their wives' attitude to their absence?

r.g.r.n. is the same.  Very hard to talk about our nethacking trials
and tribulations and successes without revealing things which may
spoil less-learned players.  But those less-learned players must
surely realise that those things will be talked about in this club?
